"Branches of the U.S. government | USAGov (https://www.usa.gov/branches-of-government)\nciteturn9view0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://www.usa.gov/branches-of-government\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 124\nL0: 【0†GTM†www.googletagmanager.com】 【1†Skip to main content】\nL1: \nL2: 【2†Image: Close】\nL3: \nL4:   * 【3†All topics and services】\nL5:   * 【4†The U.S. and its government】\nL6:   * 【5†Government benefits】\nL7:   * 【6†Immigration and U.S. citizenship】\nL8:   * 【7†Money and credit】\nL9:   * 【8†Travel】\nL10:   * 【9†Voting and elections】\nL11:   * 【4†The U.S. and its government】\nL12:     * 【10†Buying from the U.S. government】\nL13:     * 【11†U.S. facts and figures】\nL14:     * 【12†A-Z index of U.S. government departments and agencies】\nL15:     * 【13†State and local governments】\nL16:     * current page\nL17: \nL18: Branches of government\nL19: \nL20:     * 【14†Elected officials】\nL21:     * 【15†Federal laws and regulations】\nL22:     * 【16†Indian tribes and resources for Native Americans】\nL23:     * 【17†Federal, state, territory, county, and municipal courts】\nL24: # Branches of the U.S. government\nL25: \nL26: Learn about the legislative, judicial, and executive branches of the U.S. government.\nL27: \nL28: ## What are the three branches of government?\nL29: \nL30: The Constitution of the United States divides the federal government into three branches:\nL31: \nL32:   1. Legislative\nL33:   2. Judicial\nL34:   3. Executive\nL35: \nL36: This ensures that no individual or group will have too much power.\nL37: ## [Button: Legislative branch ]\nL38: \nL39: The legislative branch is 【18†made up of Congress†www.congress.gov】:\nL40: \nL41:   * The U.S. Senate\nL42:   * The U.S. House of Representatives\nL43: \nL44: It also includes special agencies and offices that provide support services to Congress.\nL45: \nL46: The legislative branch’s key roles include:\nL47:   * Drafting proposed laws\nL48:   * Confirming or rejecting presidential nominations for heads of federal agencies, federal judges, and the Supreme Court\nL49:   * Surveying the budget\nL50:   * Having the authority to declare war\nL51: ## [Button: Judicial branch ]\nL52: \nL53: The judicial branch 【19†includes the Supreme Court†www.supremecourt.gov】 and other federal courts.\nL54: \nL55: It evaluates laws by:\nL56: \nL57:   * Interpreting the meaning of laws\nL58:   * Applying laws to individual cases\nL59:   * Deciding if laws violate the Constitution\nL60: ## [Button: Executive branch ]\nL61: \nL62: 【20†The president, the vice president, and the president's cabinet are the members of the executive branch.†www.whitehouse.gov】\nL63: \nL64: The executive branch’s key roles include:\nL65:   * The president is the head of state, leader of the executive branch, and commander in chief of the United States armed forces.\nL66:   * The vice president supports the president. If the president is unable to serve, the vice president becomes president. The vice president also presides over the U.S. Senate and breaks ties in Senate votes.\nL67:   * The cabinet members serve as advisors to the president. They include the vice president, heads of executive departments, and other high-ranking government officials. Cabinet members are nominated by the president and must be approved by the Senate.\nL68: The executive branch also includes:\nL69: \nL70:   * Executive departments\nL71:   * Independent agencies\nL72:   * Other boards, commissions, and committees\nL73: ## How each branch of government provides checks and balances\nL74: \nL75: 【21†The ability of each branch to respond to the actions of the other branches is the system of checks and balances.†bensguide.gpo.gov】\nL76: \nL77: Each branch of government can change acts of the other branches:\nL78:   * The president can veto legislation created by Congress. He or she also nominates heads of federal agencies and high court appointees.\nL79:   * Congress confirms or rejects the president's nominees. It can also remove the president from office in exceptional circumstances.\nL80:   * The Justices of the Supreme Court can overturn unconstitutional laws. These justices are nominated by the president and confirmed by the Senate.\nL81: \nL82: LAST UPDATED: March 2, 2026\nL83: \nL84: SHARE THIS PAGE:\nL85: ## Have a question?\nL86: \nL87: Ask a real person any government-related question for free. They will get you the answer or let you know where to find it.\nL88: \nL89: 【22†Call USAGov】\nL90: \nL91: 【23†Chat with USAGov】\nL92: \nL93: 【24†Top】\nL94: \nL95: ### Government information\nL96: \nL97:   * 【3†All topics and services】\nL98:   * 【12†Directory of U.S. government agencies and departments】\nL99:   * 【25†Branches of government】\nL100: \nL101: ### About us\nL102: \nL103:   * 【26†About USAGov】\nL104:   * 【27†Report a website issue】\nL105:   * 【28†Website usage data】\nL106: ### For federal agencies\nL107: \nL108:   * 【29†Partner with us】\nL109:   * 【30†Read our blog】\nL110: #### USAGov Contact Center\nL111: \nL112: Ask USA.gov a question at\nL113: \nL114: 【22†1-844-USAGOV1 (1-844-872-4681)】\nL115: \nL116: USAGov is the official guide to government information and services\nL117: \nL118: An official website of the 【31†U.S. General Services Administration†www.gsa.gov】\nL119: \nL120: This website complies with Executive Order 【32†14224†www.federalregister.gov】. English is the official language of the U.S. Government, and the English-language version of this website is the authoritative source for all federal information contained herein.\nL121:   * 【33†Accessibility policy】\nL122:   * 【34†Privacy and security policies】\nL123:   * 【35†FOIA requests†www.gsa.gov】\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nParliament and the Government - UK Parliament (https://www.parliament.uk/about/how/role/relations-with-other-institutions/parliament-government/)\nciteturn9view1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Con