†MPs, Lords & offices home】\nL55:   * 【16†MPs†members.parliament.uk】\nL56:   * 【17†Members of the House of Lords†members.parliament.uk】\nL57:   * 【18†Government & opposition†members.parliament.uk】\nL58:   * 【19†Parliamentary offices】\nL59:   * 【20†Members' allowances】\nL60:   * 【21†Standards and financial interests】\nL61: ### Members of Parliament\nL62: \nL63: 【22†Find your MP Search for Members by name, postcode, or constituency†members.parliament.uk】 【17†Search for Members of the House of Lords Learn about their experience, knowledge and interests.†members.parliament.uk】\nL64: \nL65: ## About Parliament\nL66: \nL67: Parliament examines what the Government is doing, makes new laws, holds the power to set taxes and debates the issues of the day.\nL68: \nL69: 【2†Close】 【2†Back】 【2†Close】\nL70: ### In this section\nL71: \nL72:   * 【23†About Parliament home】\nL73:   * 【24†How Parliament works】\nL74:   * 【25†About MPs, Lords & officers】\nL75:   * 【26†Independent Complaints and Grievance Scheme (ICGS)】\nL76:   * 【27†Living Heritage】\nL77:   * 【28†Careers at Parliament】\nL78:   * 【29†Frequently Asked Questions】\nL79:   * 【30†Sustainability and environmental performance in Parliament】\nL80:   * 【31†UK Parliament's Modern Slavery Programme】\nL81: ## Get involved\nL82: \nL83: Read about how to contact an MP or Lord, petition Parliament and find out details of events in your area.\nL84: \nL85: 【2†Close】 【2†Back】 【2†Close】\nL86: \nL87: ### In this section\nL88: \nL89:   * 【32†Get involved home】\nL90:   * 【33†Contact an MP or a Member of the Lords】\nL91:   * 【34†Join a learning programme】\nL92:   * 【35†Research impact at the UK Parliament】\nL93:   * 【36†Have your say: Laws and debates】\nL94:   * 【37†Y Gornel Gymraeg】\nL95: ### Get involved with Parliament\nL96: \nL97: 【33†Contact an MP or Lord Contact your MP or a Member of the House of Lords about an issue that matters to you.】 【38†Keep up to date Sign up for the Your Parliament newsletter to find out how you can get involved.†parliament.us16.list-manage.com】\nL98: \nL99: ### It starts with action\nL100: \nL101:   * 【39†Sign or create a petition】\nL102:   * 【40†Sign up to UK Parliament Week†www.ukparliamentweek.org】\nL103: ## Visit Parliament\nL104: \nL105: Find out how to take a tour in person, join an online event, book tickets and plan a visit to UK Parliament.\nL106: \nL107: 【2†Close】 【2†Back】 【2†Close】\nL108: \nL109: ### In this section\nL110: \nL111:   * 【41†Visit Parliament home】\nL112:   * 【42†Tours】\nL113:   * 【43†Talks and events】\nL114:   * 【44†Watch debates and committees】\nL115:   * 【45†Plan your visit】\nL116:   * 【46†360° virtual tour】\nL117:   * 【47†Venue hire】\nL118:   * 【3†Parliament Shop†www.shop.parliament.uk】\nL119: ### Keep up to date\nL120: \nL121: 【48†Visit newsletter Sign up to our newsletter to receive the latest news about visiting UK Parliament.†parliament.us14.list-manage.com】\nL122: \nL123: ## Learning\nL124: \nL125: Your UK Parliament offers free, flexible support for teachers, community groups, and home educators to spark engagement and active citizenship.\nL126: \nL127: 【2†Close】 【2†Back】 【2†Close】\nL128: ### In this section\nL129: \nL130:   * 【49†Learning home†learning.parliament.uk】\nL131:   * 【50†About UK Parliament education†learning.parliament.uk】\nL132:   * 【51†Teacher resources and lesson plans†learning.parliament.uk】\nL133:   * 【52†CPD Training†learning.parliament.uk】\nL134:   * 【53†Outreach†learning.parliament.uk】\nL135:   * 【54†Visit†learning.parliament.uk】\nL136: ### Supporting teachers\nL137: \nL138: 【54†Inspire your students Book a school visit, classroom workshop or teacher-training session†learning.parliament.uk】 【55†Free teacher training resources Access videos, worksheets, lesson plans and games.†learning.parliament.uk】\nL139: \nL140: ### Keep up to date\nL141: \nL142:   * 【56†Sign up to our education newsletter†parliament.us16.list-manage.com】\nL143: \nL144: ## Shop\nL145: \nL146: Shop\nL147: \nL148: 【2†Close】 【2†Back】 【2†Close】\nL149: \nL150: ### In this section\nL151: \nL152:   * 【3†Shop home†www.shop.parliament.uk】\nL153: \nL154: 【57†UK Parliament】\nL155: # Parliament and the Government\nL156: \nL157: Parliament and the Government are different. They have different roles and do different things.\nL158: ### What is the Government?\nL159: \nL160: HM Government consists of the Prime Minister, their Cabinet and junior ministers, supported by the teams of non-political civil servants that work in government departments.\nL161: \nL162: The Government are the people responsible for running the country. The political party that wins the most seats at a 【58†General Election】 takes charge of the Government for up to five years, until the next General Election.\nL163: The leader of the winning party is appointed as Prime Minister and chooses other party members to work in the Government with them - as Cabinet ministers and junior ministers.\nL164: \nL165:   * 【59†How is a government formed? - Commons Library briefing†commonslibrary.parliament.uk】\nL166:   * 【60†List of government ministers [External site]†www.gov.uk】\nL167: ### What is Parliament?\nL168: \nL169: Parliament is there to represent our interests and make sure they are taken into account by the Government. The Government cannot make new laws or raise new taxes without Parliament's agreement.\nL170: \nL171: Parliament is made up of people we have elected and people who have been appointed. They sit in two separate Houses:\nL172:   * The House of Commons, where all the people we have elected at the General Election work, as MPs, for the next five years. This includes people in other political parties, as well as those in the winning party who were not chosen to be ministers.\nL173:   * The House of Lords, whose members are mostly appointed for life rather than elected. They have often been chosen because of their achievements and experience. Many do not belong to a political party.\nL174: Government ministers also have seats in Parliament but most of their work is done in Government departments.\nL175: \nL176:   * 【15†Lists of members of both Houses】\nL177: ### What does the Government do?\nL178: \nL179: The Government is responsible for deciding how the country is run and for managing things, day to day. They set taxes, choose what to spend public money on and decide how best to deliver public services, such as:\nL180: \nL181:   * the National Health Service\nL182:   * the police and armed forces\nL183:   * welfare benefits like the State Pension\nL184:   * the UK's energy supply\nL185: While many government powers have been delegated to the 【61†devolved institutions】 in Scotland, Wales and Northern Ireland, only the UK Government can speak on behalf of the UK and represent us abroad.\nL186: \nL187: More about the day to day work of the Government can be found on the 【62†Government website [External site]†www.gov.uk】\nL188: ### What does Parliament do?\nL189: \nL190: Parliament's job is to look closely at the Government's plans and to monitor the way they are running things.\nL191: \nL192: Parliament works on our behalf to try to make sure that Government decisions are:\nL193:   * open and transparent – by questioning ministers and requesting information\nL194:   * workable and efficient – by examining new proposals closely and suggesting improvements, checking how public money is being spent and tracking how new laws are working out in practice\nL195:   * fair and non-discriminatory – by checking that they comply with equalities and human rights laws and by speaking up on behalf of affected individuals\nL196: Members of both Houses of Parliament can speak up for us if a government department or agency treats us unfairly.\nL197: \nL198: Government ministers are required to come to Parliament regularly to answer questions, respond to issues raised in debates and keep both Houses informed of any important decisions they take. In this way, Parliament can hold the Government to account for its actions.\nL199: \nL200: 【63†Find out more about how Parliament checks on the work of Government】\nL201: ### What is the Opposition?\nL202: \nL203: The Opposition works in Parliament. After a General Election, the largest non-government party in the House of Commons becomes the Official Opposition. The Leader of the Opposition takes the lead role in questioning the Prime Minister when they come to Parliament.\nL204: \nL205: The Leader of the Opposition chooses a team – known as the Shadow Cabinet – who take the lead in questioning other Government ministers when they come to Parliament.\nL206:   * 【64†List of HM Official Opposition†members.parliament.uk】\nL207: ### Who should I contact – Parliament or the Government?\nL208: \nL209: It may be neither of these in the first instance – to check other options first, see our page: 【65†Who should I contact with my issue?】\nL210: \nL211: You can contact a Government minister or office for information about:\nL212: \nL213:   * why a law is going to change\nL214:   * when a law is due to change\nL215:   * how a particular law applies to you\nL216:   * a government-run service – e.g. health services, visas and immigration, state pension or tax credits/universal credit\nL217: 【66†Contact UK Government Departments and Agencies [External site]†www.gov.uk】\nL218: \nL219: You can contact your MP or a member of the House of Lords when:\nL220: \nL221:   * you want them to raise an issue in Parliament or press the Government for action\nL222:   * you want them to support or oppose a particular Government policy\nL223:   * you are not happy with the way your complaint has been handled by a government-run service or department\nL224: \nL225: 【33†Contact your MP or a member of the House of Lords】\nL226: ## Government websites\nL227: \nL228: GOV.UK is the website for the UK government. On the site you can find information and services for citizens and businesses, detailed guidance for professionals and information on government and policy.\nL229: \nL230:   * 【62†GOV.UK (external site)†www.gov.uk】\nL231: 10 Downing Street is the office of the British Prime Minister. The office helps the Prime Minister to establish and deliver the government's overall strategy and policy priorities, and to communicate the government's policies to Parliament, the public and international audiences.\nL232: \nL233:   * 【67†10 Downing Street (external site)†www.gov.uk】\nL234: ## Jargon Buster\nL235: \nL236: ### Cabinet:\nL237: \nL238: The Cabinet is the team of 20 or so most senior ministers in the Government who are chosen by the Prime Minister to lead on specific policy areas and head up  Government Departments such as Health, Transport, or Defence.\nL239: ### Shadow Cabinet:\nL240: \nL241: The Shadow Cabinet is the team of senior spokespeople chosen by the Leader of the Opposition to mirror the Cabinet in Government.\nL242: \nL243: Each member of the shadow cabinet is appointed to lead on a specific policy area for their party and to question and challenge their counterpart in the Cabinet. In this way the Official Opposition in Parliament can present itself as an alternative government-in-waiting.\nL244: \nL245:   * 【68†Glossary A-Z】\nL246: \nL247: ## Related information\nL248: ### 【69†What happens in a hung Parliament?】\nL249: \nL250: When there is no clear winner at a General Election, it is known as a 'hung Parliament'.\nL251: \nL252: To be able to run the Government, a party needs to show that it has the ‘confidence’ of the House of Commons: that is, that more than half (a majority) of the MPs will support it in key votes, such as those on taxes and spending.\nL253: \nL254: This majority can include support from other political parties, whether or not there is a formal coalition agreement.\nL255:   *  【70†What is a confidence motion?】\nL256: ## Further information\nL257: \nL258: ### Commons Library briefing\nL259: \nL260: The House of Commons Library produces briefing papers to inform MPs and their staff of key issues. The papers contain factual information and a range of opinions on each subject, and aim to be politically impartial.\nL261: \nL262:   * 【71†The Ministerial Code: Commons Library briefing paper†commonslibrary.parliament.uk】\nL263:   * 【72†Ministers in the 2015, 2017 and 2019 Conservative Governments: Commons Library briefing paper†researchbriefings.parliament.uk】\nL264: ###### Popular\nL265: \nL266:   * 【73†What's on in Parliament†whatson.parliament.uk】\nL267:   * 【74†Petitions†petition.parliament.uk】\nL268:   * 【28†Careers at Parliament】\nL269:   * 【75†Commons Media Relations】\nL270:   * 【76†Lords Press Office】\nL271:   * 【77†Contact us】\nL272: \nL273: ###### Research\nL274: \nL275:   * 【78†Commons Library†commonslibrary.parliament.uk】\nL276:   * 【79†Lords Library†lordslibrary.parliament.uk】\nL277:   * 【80†POST†post.parliament.uk】\nL278: \nL279: ###### Search Parliament\nL280: \nL281: [Input]\nL282: \nL283: [Input]\nL284: \nL285: ###### Watch Parliament Live\nL286: \nL287: 【81†Parliament Live TV†parliamentlive.tv】\nL288: ###### Follow Parliament\nL289: \nL290: ###### Legal\nL291: \nL292:   * 【82†Accessibility statement】\nL293:   * 【31†Modern slavery statement】\nL294:   * 【83†About this site】\nL295:   * 【84†Privacy notice】\nL296:   * 【85†Cookie policy】\nL297:   * 【2†Cookie settings】\nL298: \nL299: © UK Parliament 2026\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nCIVIL SERVICE Definition & Meaning - Merriam-Webster (https://www.merriam-webster.com/dictionary/civil%20service)\nciteturn9view2 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 6 days ago; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://www.merriam-webster.com/dictionary/civil%20service\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 160\nL0: 【0†iframe†www.googletagmanager.com】 【1†iframe†www.googletagmanager.com】\nL1: \nL2: [Input: Search Dictionary]\nL3: \nL4:   * 【2†Games】\nL5:   * 【3†Word of the Day】\nL6:   * 【4†Grammar】\nL7:   * 【5†Word Finder】\nL8:   * 【6†Slang】\nL9:   * 【7†Newsletters】\nL10:   * 【8†W